## 🎯 The Duplicate Primes Attack (Breaking RSA with Common Factors)
If you have a massive, secure public key N, finding its factors using brute force is computationally impossible. However, an elite hacker can bypass the entire mathematical protection of RSA if two different servers on the internet generate public keys that accidentally share a common factor. [1, 2] 
This is not a theoretical attack—real-world global scans have discovered that hundreds of thousands of active cryptographic keys on the internet suffer from this vulnerability due to low-entropy initializations on headless embedded hardware and IoT routers. [2, 3] 
------------------------------
## 🧮 The Mathematics: The Shortcut via Euclid's Algorithm
Normally, to break an RSA key, an attacker must solve:
$$N_1 = p \times q_1$$ 
Factoring N₁ to find p is the hard part. [1] 
However, suppose another router on the internet sets up its encryption at boot time and, due to a weak pseudo-random number generator, reuses that exact same prime number p. Its public key becomes:
$$N_2 = p \times q_2$$ 
Now, a hacker has two public keys: N₁ and N₂. Instead of spending millions of years factoring them individually, the hacker uses a 2,300-year-old mathematical tool called Euclid’s Algorithm to calculate the Greatest Common Factor (GCD). [1, 2, 4] 
Calculating the $\gcd(N_1, N_2)$ takes a computer a fraction of a millisecond, even if the numbers are thousands of digits long.
$$\gcd(N_1, N_2) = p$$ 
The moment the algorithm outputs p, the hacker can immediately calculate the other hidden prime factors using simple division:
$$q_1 = \frac{N_1}{p} \quad \text{and} \quad q_2 = \frac{N_2}{p}$$ 
The hacker now possesses the hidden prime factors for both keys. They can reconstruct the private keys and decrypt all secure traffic passing through both servers without ever needing a password. [4, 5] 
